{:org/id :org-phl-scholar-guilds
 :org/name "Play-to-earn scholar guilds (Yield Guild Games, Axie scholarships, subDAO guilds)"
 :org/entity-kind :named-org
 :org/form-family :family-chain-community
 :org/category :cat-labor-coop
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :global
 :org/region-primary :region-southeast-asia
 :org/countries ["PH" "ID" "VN" "BR" "IN" "NG"]
 :org/founding-date 2020
 :org/founding-note "Founded to lend game assets to Filipinos who had lost work in the 2020 pandemic."
 :org/member-count 30000
 :org/member-count-basis "source-pinned as a HISTORICAL PEAK, not a present count: YGG reported 241 scholars in February 2021, 4,700+ by August 2021, 6,600 in November 2021 and 20,700 in February 2022 — an 8,500% year-on-year rise — with over 30,000 scholarships granted network-wide by June 2022. The play-to-earn economy that supported those numbers collapsed afterwards, and no comparable current figure was located. Recorded as a boom-and-bust community whose peak is documented and whose present size is unknown."
 :org/proxy-metric "scholarship grants issued by the guild"
 :org/proxy-bias "Cumulative grants, not active players: a scholarship issued in 2021 to someone who stopped playing in 2022 still counts. The same monotonic-artefact problem as fan club membership numbers and cumulative wallet addresses."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/registration-note "YGG is a DAO with a token and subDAOs; the scholars are neither employees nor members — they are counterparties in a revenue-share on borrowed assets, which is why this sits in labor-coop rather than online."
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Guilds lending in-game assets to players who cannot afford them, splitting earnings between scholar, manager and guild — a labour arrangement dressed as a community, and the clearest case in this corpus of a community formed by an income opportunity and dissolved with it."
 :org/related-org "org-global-daos"
 :org/source-name "Yield Guild Games scholar milestone reporting (YGG blog, Cointelegraph, Naavik)"
 :org/source-url "https://medium.com/yield-guild-games/yield-guild-games-network-smashes-major-milestone-to-onboard-over-20-000-axie-infinity-scholars-3d91f7a6c9ca"
 :org/source-date "2022"
 :org/source-tier :secondary
 :org/source-caveat "Peak-era figures published by the guild itself during a token boom; treat as self-reported."
 :org/pinned-on "2026-07-26"
 :org/data-provenance "source-pinned"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-slv-bitcoin-circular-economies
 :org/name "Bitcoin circular-economy communities (Bitcoin Beach, Bitcoin Ekasi, Bitcoin Jungle)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-chain-community
 :org/category :cat-local-place
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :regional
 :org/region-primary :region-south-america
 :org/countries ["SV" "ZA" "CR" "GT" "HN" "BR"]
 :org/founding-date 2019
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown — these are town-scale projects (El Zonte, Mossel Bay's Ekasi township, Costa Rica's Bitcoin Jungle) counted in merchants onboarded and wallet installs rather than members; both proxies overstate, since a merchant who accepted once and a wallet installed for an airdrop both persist in the count."
 :org/proxy-metric "merchants onboarded and wallet installations"
 :org/proxy-bias "Onboarding events, not continuing use. Neither figure is retired when a merchant stops accepting or a wallet goes unused, so the count only rises."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Towns where a donor-seeded project pays local wages and school stipends in bitcoin and recruits merchants to accept it, aiming at a closed local loop — a community organised around a currency experiment, with its own dispute over whether it is a community or a pilot."
 :org/related-org "org-global-local-currencies"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-global-nft-holder-communities
 :org/name "NFT and PFP holder communities (profile-picture collections, token-gated groups)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-chain-community
 :org/category :cat-online
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :global
 :org/region-primary :region-north-america
 :org/countries ["US" "GB" "JP" "KR" "SG" "AE"]
 :org/founding-date 2017
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown as people: collections publish holder counts (unique addresses holding at least one token), which is the address problem again in concentrated form — whales hold hundreds of items across many wallets, and token-gated Discords count members who have since sold. A collection's 'community size' is routinely quoted from holder counts that no one reconciles to persons."
 :org/proxy-metric "unique holding addresses and token-gated chat membership"
 :org/proxy-bias "Overstates through multi-wallet holders and stale gated-chat membership; understates where custodial platforms hold on behalf of users. Additionally the metric is PRICE-COUPLED: holder counts fall when the floor falls, so the measured community tracks the asset, not the affiliation."
 :org/legal-registration :unregistered
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Groups whose membership token is a tradable asset — entry is purchase, exit is sale, and the community's boundary is therefore a market — which makes them the clearest counter-case to every inherited or place-based form in this corpus."
 :org/related-org "org-global-chain-communities"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-global-sf-fandom-worldcon
 :org/name "Science fiction fandom, Worldcon and fanzine communities"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-fan-club
 :org/category :cat-hobby-interest
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :global
 :org/region-primary :region-north-america
 :org/countries ["US" "GB" "JP" "FI" "IE" "CA" "CN"]
 :org/founding-date 1939
 :org/founding-note "The first Worldcon was held in New York in 1939; the Hugo Awards began in 1953."
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown across fandom as a whole — Worldcon memberships are counted per convention (typically several thousand attending plus supporting members who vote without attending), and each year's convention is run by a different volunteer committee, so there is no continuing membership roll to count."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/registration-note "Each Worldcon is organized by a separate incorporated committee that dissolves after the event, while the World Science Fiction Society exists only as a constitution — an institution with rules, elections and no permanent organization."
 :org/governance-model :democratic
 :org/mission-statement "The oldest continuously organized fandom, which runs an annual award elected by paying members under a written constitution, chooses the location of future conventions by member vote, and sustains a parallel amateur press — a fan community that built formal democratic machinery decades before online fandom existed."
 :org/coverage-note "Included as the counterpart to the agency-owned fan club: here the fans own the institution and the creators do not."
 :org/related-org "org-jpn-artist-fan-clubs"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-jpn-kouenkai-athlete
 :org/name "後援会 (Japanese patron associations for athletes, sumo stables and politicians)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-fan-club
 :org/category :cat-sports
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :national
 :org/region-primary :region-asia-east
 :org/countries ["JP"]
 :org/founding-date 1900
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown — 後援会 exist for individual rikishi, athletes, entertainers and almost every politician, funded by subscriptions and banquet attendance; political kouenkai are partially visible through political funding reports, sporting ones are not counted anywhere."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/registration-note "Political 後援会 must file as political organizations and are therefore visible in funding disclosures; the sporting and entertainment versions are private associations with no filing duty — the same social form split across two registration regimes by what it supports."
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Subscription supporter associations attached to one person rather than a club — paying for a rikishi's tegata and stable expenses, an athlete's travel, or a politician's constituency machine, and receiving proximity in return."
 :org/coverage-note "Recorded across sport and politics deliberately: the political kouenkai is the same institution as the sumo one, and treating them as unrelated would hide that."
 :org/related-org "org-global-football-supporters-branches"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
